# Chapter 1: the estate
#
# Deliberator is an IntelliJ plugin plus a daemon (`deliberatord`) that does the work. You are reading a scenario: plain text,
# one step per `###` line. The comments teach, the steps do it; run them and the result of each step appears under it, here in
# the editor, and as files under $DELIBERATOR_HOME/workspaces/<ws>/sessions/<session>/runs/.
#
# How to run (in the IDE): right-click → Deliberator Scenario → Run Scenario runs every step; Ctrl+Enter runs the step at the
# caret only; the green ▷ in the gutter of a `###` line offers "this step / from here / all". `bb tour:ide` opened you here;
# `bb tour` runs every chapter in order in a terminal (`--only 3` one chapter): the tour is also the integration test.
#
# The estate is the small AWS system Deliberator is built against, all on this machine: an SQS inbox -> service A (Spring
# Boot, DynamoDB) -> an EventBridge bus -> service B (acks A over HTTP, writes a row in Postgres) and a Lambda logger. AWS is
# Floci, an emulator in a container. This chapter brings it all up and looks at what is running.

### shell estate-up
# A `shell` step runs its body with bash, here from the repository root (`@cwd ..`: relative to this file). The output streams
# in while it runs (about 2 minutes the first time: the service image, Maven, Terraform; ~1 minute after that). It builds the
# services, starts Floci and Postgres (`runtime.ensure`), applies Terraform onto Floci (queues, table, bus, Lambda, parameters,
# secrets, ECS task definitions), adopts service A and B from their task definitions and starts their containers.
# A non-zero exit fails the step; the whole transcript is kept as response.txt of the step.
# @cwd ..
# @timeout 15m
# @assert out contains estate up in
bb estate:up --ws "$DLB_WS"

### op service.list services
# An `op` step calls one operation of the daemon (the same ones `dlb` and the IDE use; `dlb help` lists them all) with the
# JSON body as arguments; `ws` and `session` default to this run's. `$` is the answer: here one row per adopted service.
# In the IDE: Ctrl+Alt+Enter on this step (Peek Response) shows that answer as JSON; Enter on a line adds an `@assert` for it.
# @assert $[0].name == a
# @assert $[0].status == running
# @assert $[1].name == b
# @assert $[1].status == running

### op runtime.status containers
# The containers of this workspace: Floci, Postgres, service-a, service-b, with health and host ports. The same table is the
# "Runtime" part of the Deliberator tool window (Workspace tab, right sidebar).
# @assert $.podman.reachable == true

### op env.describe local
# The `local` environment: the AWS endpoint (Floci, fake credentials) and the targets scenarios use by name: `queue/inbox`,
# `table/messages`, `service/a`, `log/service-a`, `db/main` ... Chapter 2 sends an order through them.
# @assert $.env.name == local
{"name": "local"}
